= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <previsible sigma-algebra> $\mathcal P$ on $\Omega\times[0,\infty)$ is generated by $E\times\{0\}$ with $E\in\mathcal F_0$ and by $E\times(s,t]$ with $E\in\mathcal F_s$, $0\leq s<t$. Equivalently it is the smallest sigma-algebra making every left-continuous <adapted process> measurable. A <previsible process> is a process measurable for $\mathcal P$.

A bounded <simple predictable process> has finitely many time intervals and bounded coefficients known at their left endpoints. One may use deterministic endpoints, or the customary more general <elementary predictable process with stopping-time intervals>:
$$
H_s=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}h_j\mathbf1_{(T_j,T_{j+1}]}(s),\qquad0=T_0\leq\cdots\leq T_m=\infty,\qquad h_j\in L^\infty(\mathcal F_{T_j}).
$$
A bounded $\mathcal F_0$-measurable value at time zero may be added; it does not affect integration against a <continuous martingale>. A process with finite support is included by adding a zero coefficient on its terminal interval. Both versions of simple processes satisfy the arguments below. The elementary <stochastic integral> is
$$
\boxed{(H\cdot M)_t=\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}h_j\bigl(M_{t\wedge T_{j+1}}-M_{t\wedge T_j}\bigr).}
$$
When a <stopping time> is infinite, use the terminal value $M_\infty$, which exists in $L^2$ and almost surely for an <L2-bounded continuous martingale>. Refining intervals leaves this definition unchanged.

For a <continuous local martingale> $M$, its <quadratic variation> $[M]$ is the unique continuous adapted nondecreasing process starting at zero such that $(M_t-M_0)^2-[M]_t$ is a <local martingale>. It is predictable because it is continuous and adapted. To recover it from the path, use refining deterministic grids, for example $t_k^{(n)}=k2^{-n}$, and form
$$
Q_t^{(n)}=\sum_{k\geq0}\bigl(M_{t\wedge t_{k+1}^{(n)}}-M_{t\wedge t_k^{(n)}}\bigr)^2.
$$
Only finitely many increments are nonzero on a compact time interval. Then
$$
\boxed{Q^{(n)}\longrightarrow[M]\text{ uniformly on compacts in probability}.}
$$
In particular each fixed-time sum converges in probability, and a suitable subsequence converges uniformly on compact intervals almost surely. The partial last grid interval is included in this path approximation.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the representation from part (a), and put $\Delta_jM=M_{T_{j+1}}-M_{T_j}$, $U_j=h_j\Delta_jM$ and $N=H\cdot M$. The <optional stopping theorem> in the form needed here says that for a <uniformly integrable martingale> $L$ and <stopping times> $\sigma\leq\tau$, including terminal infinite times, $\mathbb E[L_\tau\mid\mathcal F_\sigma]=L_\sigma$. An <L2-bounded continuous martingale> is <uniformly integrable> and has the required terminal value.

Consequently $\mathbb E[\Delta_jM\mid\mathcal F_{T_j}]=0$. Conditioning on $\mathcal F_t$ and distinguishing whether $T_j\leq t$ gives
$$
\mathbb E[U_j\mid\mathcal F_t]=h_j\bigl(M_{t\wedge T_{j+1}}-M_{t\wedge T_j}\bigr).
$$
Thus $N_t=\mathbb E[\sum_jU_j\mid\mathcal F_t]$ is a <martingale>. Its paths are continuous, since each stopped increment is continuous and zero until its left endpoint.

For $i<j$, $U_i$ is $\mathcal F_{T_j}$-measurable, so the same conditional identity yields $\mathbb E(U_iU_j)=0$. These <martingale-difference orthogonality> relations also hold for the unweighted increments. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E N_\infty^2&=\sum_j\mathbb E\bigl(h_j^2(\Delta_jM)^2\bigr)\\
&\leq\|H\|_\infty^2\sum_j\mathbb E(\Delta_jM)^2
=\|H\|_\infty^2\mathbb E(M_\infty-M_0)^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $N_\infty=\sum_jU_j\in L^2$. By conditional Jensen, $\mathbb E N_t^2\leq\mathbb E N_\infty^2$ for every $t$, so
$$
\boxed{H\cdot M\in\mathcal M_c^2,\qquad\mathbb E(H\cdot M)_\infty^2\leq\|H\|_\infty^2\mathbb E(M_\infty-M_0)^2.}
$$
This proves the norm bound without presupposing the <Itô isometry> requested next.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

First justify optional sampling for the square-minus-bracket process. The <Doob L2 maximal inequality> gives $\mathbb E\sup_{t\geq0}|M_t|^2\leq4\mathbb E|M_\infty|^2<\infty$. Stop the <local martingale> $M^2-[M]$ using a localizing sequence and bounded bracket levels. The stopped expectation identity, followed by Fatou, bounds $\mathbb E[M]_\infty$ by the expected squared path supremum. Hence $M^2-[M]$ has an integrable absolute supremum, and localization with conditional dominated convergence makes it a <uniformly integrable martingale>. The <optional stopping theorem> stated in part (b) is therefore applicable to both $M$ and $M^2-[M]$.

For ordered <stopping times> $\sigma\leq\tau$, expand the squared increment and condition on $\mathcal F_\sigma$. The mean increment is zero, so
$$
\mathbb E[(M_\tau-M_\sigma)^2\mid\mathcal F_\sigma]=\mathbb E[M_\tau^2-M_\sigma^2\mid\mathcal F_\sigma]=\mathbb E([M]_\tau-[M]_\sigma\mid\mathcal F_\sigma).
$$
This is the <conditional bracket isometry for stopped martingale increments>. Apply it with $\sigma=T_j$, $\tau=T_{j+1}$ and multiply by the bounded $\mathcal F_{T_j}$-measurable variable $h_j^2$. The orthogonality proved in part (b) gives the <Itô isometry>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E(H\cdot M)_\infty^2=\sum_j\mathbb E\bigl[h_j^2([M]_{T_{j+1}}-[M]_{T_j})\bigr]=\mathbb E\int_0^\infty H_s^2\,d[M]_s.}
$$

To identify the whole bracket process, let $N=H\cdot M$ and $C_t=\int_0^tH_s^2\,d[M]_s$. For deterministic $s<t$ and $E\in\mathcal F_s$, apply the proved isometry to the simple integrand $\mathbf1_E\mathbf1_{(s,t]}H$. It yields
$$
\mathbb E[\mathbf1_E(N_t-N_s)^2]=\mathbb E[\mathbf1_E(C_t-C_s)].
$$
Thus $\mathbb E[(N_t-N_s)^2\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E[C_t-C_s\mid\mathcal F_s]$. Since $N$ is a <martingale>, the cross term $2N_s(N_t-N_s)$ has conditional mean zero. Consequently $N^2-C$ is a <martingale>. The process $C$ is continuous, adapted, nondecreasing and starts at zero, so uniqueness in the definition of <quadratic variation> proves
$$
\boxed{[H\cdot M]_t=\int_0^tH_s^2\,d[M]_s=(H^2\cdot[M])_t.}
$$
This deduction uses only the proved elementary isometry, not a previously assumed formula for the <quadratic variation> of <stochastic integrals>.

